;;; `naf-upward-closure.ein` under the singleton writeback's lever — its
;;; lever row, where `dead` not being upward-closed under `absent` turned into a
;;; certified `Contradiction`. Regression `naf-nowb-L`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; Below this header the program is `../naf-upward-closure.ein`'s, its `:why`
;;; texts aside. The lever, `:enable-singleton-writeback false` on a
;;; `(config …)` line, is no flag of Ein: it wrote into root, which no route
;;; does, and R never read it. That file's header is the argument (*forbid*).
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `bad` concludes `(false)` from `(absent (q ?x))` over `q`, which a
;;; commitment can still extend, so the program is refused at load, the error
;;; naming the replacement (*forbid*, Q-M1e2.5). Read without the refusal
;;; (*respect*, the row's alternative), the lattice is
;;;
;;;   alive₀          = {(p A), (q A)}
;;;   {(p A)}         is dead   — `bad` fires, (q A) absent
;;;   {(p A), (q A)}  is alive  — the guard's witness is present
;;;
;;; and its one maximal state is `{(p A), (q A)}`: `Solution`, k = 1.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The walk's two runs, which the regression records:
;;;
;;;   default  Solution k = 1, `{(q A)}` — the same wrong model as
;;;            `../naf-upward-closure.ein`'s: the kill cache wrote `(not (p A))`
;;;            with no premises.
;;;   -L       `No solution — the constraints are contradictory`, unsat core
;;;            `(p A)`, `exhausted = true`. With neither the cache nor the
;;;            writeback, `{(p A)}` died at layer 1 and its width-1 no-good
;;;            pruned the live superset as if it were dead. `{(q A)}` survived
;;;            and was not recorded either — `(p A)` was still proposed, so it
;;;            was not `complete` — and the empty layer 2 ended the run with the
;;;            dead core as the program's. Two mechanisms, then: the dead set not
;;;            upward-closed under `absent`, and the unrecorded survivor.
;;;
;;; The engine refuses the program at load: `ein solve` exits 1 naming `bad`
;;; over `q`, and `ein saturate` still saturates. No `:expect`, then.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation is-a T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")

(is-a A T)

(rule bad ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has p without q")

(query
  :goal   (is-a ?x T))
